(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-pigmented-skin-lesions-vignettes"]={"title":"Pigmented Skin Lesions Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 46-year-old African American woman has dozens of smooth firm dark brown papules 1 to 3 mm across over both cheeks and the neck, present for years. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acquired lentigines of the face and neck","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans","Multiple blue naevi of the face","Dysplastic naevi of the face and neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — identical to small seborrheic keratoses, commonest in this group, with females affected more than males. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with freckles is unsure whether her lesions are freckles or age spots. Which question best separates the two?","choices":["Whether the lesions itch or are otherwise symptomatic","Whether the lesions fade when sun exposure stops","Whether the lesions are located on the face or trunk","Whether the lesions are raised or flat to the touch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ephelides fade with reduced exposure and lentigines do not. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman notices she has fewer moles than she did in her thirties. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Numbers of naevi continue to rise throughout life, so some have been missed","Sun protection over the years has caused the existing naevi to fade away","Naevi that disappear have usually transformed into an unrecognised melanoma","Numbers of common acquired naevi peak in the thirties and decline afterwards"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they develop slowly after birth, enlarge, stabilise and then regress. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has about forty moles, most 3 to 5 mm, evenly coloured, round and sharply demarcated. He asks whether he has too many. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Numbers are unrelated to melanoma risk, so no monitoring or sun protection is required at all","Numbers this high define dysplastic naevus syndrome, which requires over one hundred by adolescence","Numbers peak in the thirties and then decline, but melanoma risk rises with the number, so sun protection and monitoring matter","Numbers continue to rise throughout life, and any patient with over twenty naevi needs them all excised early"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — light skin tone and a tendency to sunburn increase the number. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"The parents of a child with naevus spilus ask about follow-up. Which is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Observation with periodic clinical evaluation, plus sun protection counselling","Excision of the whole patch before adolescence to eliminate the melanoma risk","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and total spine at the first visit","Biopsy of each darker macule within the patch to exclude atypical features"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with facial papules typical of dermatosis papulosa nigrans has one lesion that looks different from the rest. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat all the lesions with cryotherapy and review the atypical one afterwards","Reassure him, since every lesion in this condition is benign without exception","Arrange dermoscopy of the whole face and repeat it again in twelve months","Biopsy that lesion, since biopsy is indicated where the diagnosis is uncertain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is otherwise clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with several dysplastic naevi asks where they are most likely to appear. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They are common on sun-exposed skin surfaces","They are common on the palms, soles and mucosae","They are common on the dorsal hands, scalp and sacrum","They are common on the face and neck in darker skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are at least 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders and variable pigmentation. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has generalised darkly pigmented lentigines over much of his body. Which additional consideration does this raise?","choices":["Dysplastic naevus syndrome should be considered, and every lesion biopsied","An internal malignancy should be considered, and urgent screening arranged","An inherited disorder should be considered, as with LAMB and myxoma syndrome","Neurocutaneous melanosis should be considered, and brain imaging arranged"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a partial or generalised lentigo is what prompts that question. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a 2 cm blue-grey nodule on the sacral region suspected to be a cellular blue naevus. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Clinical diagnosis and observation, since blue naevi are always benign lesions","Cryotherapy for cosmetic removal, since the lesion sits on a visible area","Magnetic resonance imaging of the spine, given the sacral location of it","Biopsy, since diagnosis is clinical for small lesions but by biopsy for larger ones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — observation follows, with biopsy or excision if changes are noted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"The parents of a child with a very large congenital naevus feel isolated and overwhelmed. Which support does the lecture name?","choices":["Referral to a genetics service for testing of both parents and the child","Referral to psychiatry for management of the parental distress specifically","Referral to palliative care for early goals-of-care discussion with the family","Counselling and support groups for families and patients with large naevi"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is named alongside the surgical and observational decision. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man asks whether treating his solar lentigines will reduce his skin cancer risk. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Treating them is cosmetic; reducing risk depends on sun protection and surveillance for other lesions","Treating them removes the precancerous cells and reduces the risk substantially over time","Treating them has no cosmetic or medical value, so no intervention should be offered at all","Treating them is essential, since untreated lesions progress to melanoma within a decade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesions mark sun damage rather than causing the cancers associated with it. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man asks why his freckles faded each winter as a child but the brown spots he has now do not. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Lentigines fade more slowly, over several years rather than one season","Lentigines are raised rather than flat, so they retain their pigment","Lentigines occur only on sun-protected skin, which never lightens","Lentigines do not fade when sun exposure stops, unlike ephelides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that behaviour is the main clinical distinction between the two. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with numerous solar lentigines asks whether the birth control she took for years contributed. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Birth control use raises the risk of melanoma within these lesions specifically","Birth control use is among the named associations, alongside older age, sun damage, ephelides and tanning","Birth control use is unrelated, since solar lentigines are caused by sun exposure and nothing else","Birth control use is the principal cause, and stopping it will make the lesions fade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — 90% of people have solar lentigines by the age of fifty. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with stable vitiligo that has not changed in four years asks about surgery. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Surgery is offered only where disease is actively spreading across the body surface","Tissue or cellular grafting is an option, since surgery is reserved for highly stable disease","Surgery is never used in vitiligo, since the grafted skin depigments in the same way","Surgery is offered first line in all patients, ahead of phototherapy and topical therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — his four stable years are exactly the criterion. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has a sharply circumscribed jet-black papule 5 mm across on the thigh, with faint blue-grey shading at one edge. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Spitz naevus of the lower extremity","A dysplastic naevus of the lower extremity","Pigmented spindle cell naevus, also called Reed naevus","A blue naevus of the lower extremity or the thigh"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — commonest in the thirties, females more than males, on the lower extremities and mainly the thigh. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with several solar lentigines wants them removed. Which options are appropriate?","choices":["Retinoids, cryotherapy or quality-switched laser","Topical fluorouracil, imiquimod or tirbanibulin","Excision with clear margins under local anaesthetic","Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy three times weekly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary, and removal is for cosmetic reasons. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel warty and look pasted onto the skin. She is worried they are cancers. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They are dysplastic naevi requiring excision because of the risk of melanoma","They are solar lentigines requiring cryotherapy for cosmetic improvement","They are benign seborrheic keratoses, diagnosed clinically, and management is supportive","They are premalignant lesions requiring biopsy of each one before any reassurance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they are common in older adults and easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 29-year-old fair-skinned woman has a 4 mm naevus on the shoulder that is very dark brown, almost black, unlike her other moles. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Very dark brown or black on light skin is suspicious and warrants further assessment","Dark colour is expected in a common acquired naevus and needs no further attention","Dark colour indicates a blue naevus, which is benign and needs only observation","Dark colour indicates a seborrheic keratosis, which is benign and needs no action"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — common acquired naevi are usually skin coloured, brown or pink with homogenous colour. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has several lesions with the appearance of Spitz naevi. Which additional consideration does this raise?","choices":["Multiple lesions suggest an inherited disorder such as LAMB or myxoma syndrome","Multiple lesions can be associated with a familial cancer syndrome","Multiple lesions indicate that neurocutaneous melanosis is likely present","Multiple lesions define dysplastic naevus syndrome in a child of this age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — diagnosis is by biopsy or wide excision, and management is by excision. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has a firm pink dome-shaped papule on the forearm. His parents ask whether it could be on his palm or in his mouth too. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Spitz naevi occur only on the dorsal hands, scalp and sacrum","Spitz naevi occur only on the lower extremity, mainly the thigh","Spitz naevi spare the palms, soles and mucous membranes","Spitz naevi occur most commonly on the palms and the soles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they occur on face, neck, trunk and extremities. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman asks how a mole she was born with differs from the ones that appeared later. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Congenital naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursors migrating along neurovascular bundles; acquired naevi arise from junctional melanocytes","Congenital naevi arise from junctional melanocytes in the epidermis; acquired naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursor cells","Congenital naevi arise from keratinocytes in the basal layer; acquired naevi arise from dermal fibroblasts within the skin","Congenital naevi arise from hair follicle precursors; acquired naevi arise from vascular endothelium in the dermis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic naevi are the group with atypical architectural and cytologic features. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with vitiligo says he has stopped going out because of how his skin looks, and that his doctor called it purely cosmetic. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["The condition is indeed cosmetic, so treatment is optional and reassurance is enough","The condition indicates an underlying malignancy, which explains how unwell he feels","The condition will resolve spontaneously within a year, so no treatment is needed","The psychological and social impact is real, and psychological intervention is part of management"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — low self-esteem and poor body image are named explicitly. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has vitiligo involving about 3% of her body surface, mostly on the face and neck. Which topical option best suits those sites?","choices":["A topical calcineurin inhibitor such as tacrolimus or pimecrolimus","A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily to the face","A topical depigmenting agent such as hydroquinone or an alpha-hydroxy acid","A topical retinoid applied nightly to the affected areas of skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they suit areas where steroids cannot be applied, though they carry an increased cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with dysplastic naevi has one lesion that her partner says has changed shape over three months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Excise all of her naevi, since the risk rises with the number present","Reassure her, since dysplastic naevi are benign and do not change at all","Biopsy that lesion, since all changing or developing lesions are biopsied","Photograph it and review the appearance again in twelve months' time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — excision follows where there is concern for melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"The parents of a child with a large congenital naevus on the back ask whether it can be removed. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["It should be treated with cryotherapy or laser rather than surgery in all young children","It depends on melanoma risk, cosmesis and function; the goal is to remove as much as possible while preserving function","It must be excised completely before school age in every case, regardless of any graft considerations","It should never be excised, since surgery on a congenital naevus increases the risk of melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — where there is little skin for a graft site, observation may be the better option. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man of dark-skinned Asian background has facial papules typical of dermatosis papulosa nigrans and asks whether they will worsen. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["They will resolve on their own within two years if sun protection is used consistently","They are contagious to close contacts, so household members should be examined too","They are benign and best left untreated, with removal available if he wants it for cosmetic reasons","They will progress to melanoma if untreated, so excision of every lesion is advised now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — excision, curettage or laser are the options, avoiding cryotherapy. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A newborn has a flat brown patch 14 cm across over the lower back, with a slightly uneven border and a pebbly surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A dysplastic melanocytic naevus","A large congenital melanocytic naevus","A naevus spilus of the lower back","A café-au-lait macule of the trunk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — evident at birth or shortly after, and the larger the lesion the higher the melanoma risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 27-year-old white man whose father had melanoma has over one hundred naevi, many at least 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders and variable tan to brown colour. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dysplastic naevus syndrome","Multiple common acquired naevi","Generalised lentigines with an inherited association","Multiple naevus spilus lesions across the trunk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — over one hundred naevi by adolescence, with family history as a risk factor. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with vitiligo asks whether PUVA would work better than the narrow band ultraviolet B he has been offered. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["PUVA is preferred, because it produces faster repigmentation across a much larger body surface area","Neither is used in vitiligo, since phototherapy risks worsening the depigmented patches","Both are equivalent, so the choice can be made purely on cost and on availability","Narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred, because PUVA carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — combining phototherapy with topical therapy is described as ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl has a lesion clinically suggestive of Spitz naevus. Why can it not simply be observed?","choices":["It is contagious and can spread to other family members through contact","It sometimes resembles melanoma, so diagnosis is by biopsy or wide excision","It always transforms into melanoma if it is left in place beyond adolescence","It grows continuously and will reach several centimetres if left untreated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — management is then by excision. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
